## 2. Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)

### Core Algorithm & Conflict Resolution

CRDTs use mathematical properties to guarantee convergence without coordination.

**Basic principle - CRDT text structure:**
```javascript
// Each character has a unique, immutable identifier
class CRDTChar {
  constructor(id, value, leftId, rightId) {
    this.id = id;           // Globally unique (siteId + counter)
    this.value = value;     // The character
    this.leftId = leftId;   // Position relative to left neighbor
    this.rightId = rightId; // Position relative to right neighbor
    this.deleted = false;   // Tombstone for deletions
  }
}

// Example: "Hi"
// [
//   { id: "A:1", value: "H", leftId: null, rightId: "A:2" },
//   { id: "A:2", value: "i", leftId: "A:1", rightId: null }
// ]

// User B inserts "e" between them:
// { id: "B:1", value: "e", leftId: "A:1", rightId: "A:2" }
// Result: "Hei" (deterministic ordering)
```

**Conflict resolution is automatic:**
```javascript
// Two users insert at same position
// User A: insert "X" between "H" and "i"
// User B: insert "Y" between "H" and "i"

// Both operations succeed independently:
// A's view: { id: "A:3", value: "X", leftId: "A:1", rightId: "A:2" }
// B's view: { id: "B:2", value: "Y", leftId: "A:1", rightId: "A:2" }

// When merged, deterministic ordering (e.g., by siteId):
// If A < B: "HXYi"
// If B < A: "HYXi"
// All clients converge to same order without coordination
```

**Key properties:**
- **Commutative**: Operations can be applied in any order
- **Associative**: Grouping doesn't matter
- **Idempotent**: Applying same operation twice has no extra effect
- **No central authority needed**: Peer-to-peer capable

### Latency Characteristics

**True peer-to-peer:**
```
User A types → appears immediately (0ms)
              ↓
         Broadcasts to peers directly
              ↓
User B receives → applies immediately (network latency only)

Typical latency: 20-100ms (direct peer connection)
vs OT: 100-300ms (client → server → client)
```

**User experience advantages:**
- No server round-trip for confirmation
- Lower latency in good network conditions
- No "correction" flickers (operations never transformed)

**Challenges:**
- Interleaving: concurrent edits at same position may interleave characters
- Less intuitive behavior in some conflict scenarios

### Scalability Limits

**Users per document:**
- **Practical limit: 200+ concurrent editors**
- No central bottleneck
- Each client processes operations independently
- Limited by network bandwidth and client processing power

**Document size:**
- **Challenge: metadata overhead**
- Each character carries unique ID and position metadata
- Memory usage: 10-50 bytes per character (vs 1-2 bytes for plain text)

```javascript
// Memory comparison
// Plain text: "Hello" = 5 bytes
// CRDT representation:
[
  { id: "A:1", value: "H", leftId: null, rightId: "A:2", deleted: false },
  { id: "A:2", value: "e", leftId: "A:1", rightId: "A:3", deleted: false },
  // ... ~40-50 bytes per character
]
// Total: ~250 bytes for "Hello"
```

**Tombstone accumulation:**
```javascript
// Deleted characters remain as tombstones
"Hello" → delete "ell" → "Ho"

// Internal representation still contains:
[
  { id: "A:1", value: "H", deleted: false },
  { id: "A:2", value: "e", deleted: true },   // Tombstone
  { id: "A:3", value: "l", deleted: true },   // Tombstone
  { id: "A:4", value: "l", deleted: true },   // Tombstone
  { id: "A:5", value: "o", deleted: false }
]
```

**Practical limits:**
- Documents with heavy editing: 100K-500K characters
- Requires periodic garbage collection
- Some implementations support compaction strategies

### Implementation Complexity

**Moderate to high complexity:**

```javascript
// Simplified CRDT implementation
class CRDTDocument {
  constructor(siteId) {
    this.siteId = siteId;
    this.counter = 0;
    this.chars = [];
  }

  // Insert is straightforward
  insert(position, value) {
    const id = `${this.siteId}:${this.counter++}`;
    const leftId = this.chars[position - 1]?.id || null;
    const rightId = this.chars[position]?.id || null;

    const char = { id, value, leftId, rightId, deleted: false };
    this.chars.splice(position, 0, char);

    return char; // Broadcast this
  }

  // Receiving remote insert
  remoteInsert(char) {
    // Find correct position based on leftId and rightId
    const position = this.findPosition(char.leftId, char.rightId);
    this.chars.splice(position, 0, char);
  }

  // The tricky part: finding correct position
  findPosition(leftId, rightId) {
    // Must handle:
    // - Concurrent inserts at same position
    // - Deleted neighbors (tombstones)
    // - Deterministic ordering for convergence

    let pos = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.chars.length; i++) {
      if (this.chars[i].id === leftId) {
        pos = i + 1;
        break;
      }
    }

    // Handle concurrent inserts with deterministic ordering
    while (pos < this.chars.length && 
           this.chars[pos].leftId === leftId &&
           this.chars[pos].id < char.id) {
      pos++;
    }

    return pos;
  }
}
```

**Challenges:**
- Position calculation can be complex
- Garbage collection strategies
- Handling rich text and nested structures
- Ensuring true convergence in all edge cases

**Advantages:**
- No transformation functions needed
- Adding new operation types is simpler
- Easier to reason about correctness

### Offline Support

**Excellent offline capabilities:**

```javascript
class CRDTOfflineClient {
  localOperations = [];

  // Work offline indefinitely
  offlineEdit(position, value) {
    const op = this.document.insert(position, value);
    this.localOperations.push(op);
    // No need to track version or base state
  }

  // Sync when online
  async syncWhenOnline() {
    // Simply send all operations
    await this.broadcast(this.localOperations);

    // Receive and apply remote operations
    const remoteOps = await this.fetchRemoteOperations();
    for (const op of remoteOps) {
      this.document.remoteInsert(op);
    }

    // Guaranteed to converge, regardless of order
    this.localOperations = [];
  }
}
```

**Advantages:**
- Can work offline for days or weeks
- No complex conflict resolution on sync
- Operations can be applied in any order
- Natural fit for local-first software

**Real-world pattern:**
```
Day 1: User A works offline, makes 1000 edits
Day 2: User B works offline, makes 500 edits
Day 3: Both come online
       → Exchange operations
       → Both converge to same state automatically
```

### Real-World Examples

**Figma (modified CRDT):**
- Uses CRDT-like approach for multiplayer cursors and selections
- Custom implementation optimized for design tools
- Handles 100+ simultaneous viewers/editors

**Actual CRDT implementations:**
- **Automerge**: General-purpose CRDT library
- **Yjs**: High-performance CRDT for text and rich data
- **TinyBase**: Lightweight CRDT for structured data

**Reported experiences:**
- Excellent offline support
- Memory overhead concerns for very large documents
- Occasional "interleaving" surprises users
- Strong eventual consistency valued for distributed teams

