## 3. Hybrid Approach (Y.js)

### Core Algorithm & Conflict Resolution

Y.js combines CRDT principles with optimizations that address pure CRDT limitations.

**Key innovations:**

```javascript
// 1. Block-based structure instead of character-based
class YBlock {
  constructor(id, content) {
    this.id = id;              // Unique block ID
    this.content = content;    // Array of characters (not individual IDs)
    this.left = null;          // Left neighbor block
    this.right = null;         // Right neighbor block
  }
}

// Instead of:
// [H][e][l][l][o] (5 CRDT items)

// Y.js uses:
// [Hello] (1 block containing 5 characters)

// Reduces metadata overhead by ~10x
```

**Conflict resolution:**
```javascript
// Combines CRDT convergence with practical optimizations
class YText {
  // When two users type in sequence at same position
  insert(position, text) {
    // Try to extend existing block (common case)
    const block = this.findBlockAt(position);

    if (block.canExtend(position, this.clientId)) {
      // Fast path: append to existing block
      block.content += text;
      return { type: 'extend', blockId: block.id, text };
    } else {
      // Create new block (concurrent edit)
      const newBlock = new YBlock(this.generateId(), text);
      this.insertBlock(newBlock, position);
      return { type: 'insert', block: newBlock };
    }
  }
}
```

**Structural sharing:**
```javascript
// Y.js uses efficient data structures
class YDocument {
  constructor() {
    // Shared types that can be nested
    this.types = {
      text: new YText(),
      map: new YMap(),
      array: new YArray(),
      xml: new YXmlFragment()
    };
  }
}

// Example: Rich text document
const doc = new Y.Doc();
const ytext = doc.getText('content');
const ymap = doc.getMap('metadata');

// Supports complex structures efficiently
ytext.insert(0, 'Hello', { bold: true });
ymap.set('author', 'Alice');
```

### Latency Characteristics

**Optimized for real-world networks:**

```javascript
// Update batching and compression
class YjsConnection {
  pendingUpdates = [];

  sendUpdate(update) {
    this.pendingUpdates.push(update);

    // Batch updates over 50ms window
    if (!this.batchTimer) {
      this.batchTimer = setTimeout(() => {
        const merged = Y.mergeUpdates(this.pendingUpdates);
        const compressed = Y.encodeStateAsUpdate(merged);

        // Typical compression: 70-90% size reduction
        this.websocket.send(compressed);

        this.pendingUpdates = [];
        this.batchTimer = null;
      }, 50);
    }
  }
}
```

**Latency profile:**
```
Local edit:     0ms (immediate)
Batch window:   0-50ms (configurable)
Network:        20-100ms (peer-to-peer or via server)
Apply remote:   <1ms (efficient merge)

Total perceived latency: 20-150ms
```

**User experience:**
- Smooth local editing (no lag)
- Efficient network usage (batching + compression)
- Rare conflicts handled gracefully
- Cursor positions tracked accurately

### Scalability Limits

**Users per document:**
- **Tested: 200+ concurrent editors**
- Peer-to-peer: limited by network topology
- Server-mediated: limited by server bandwidth

**Document size:**
- **Practical limit: 1-10 million characters**
- Block-based structure reduces overhead
- Efficient encoding reduces memory usage

**Performance characteristics:**
```javascript
// Memory usage comparison (approximate)
// Plain text: 1 byte/char
// Pure CRDT: 40-50 bytes/char
// Y.js: 3-8 bytes/char (depending on edit patterns)

// Example: 100,000 character document
// Plain text:  100 KB
// Pure CRDT:   4-5 MB
// Y.js:        300-800 KB
```

**Garbage collection:**
```javascript
// Y.js supports efficient GC
const doc = new Y.Doc({ gc: true });

// Deleted content is actually removed (not just tombstoned)
// But maintains enough history for conflict resolution
doc.getText().delete(0, 100); // Actually frees memory

// Can also snapshot and compact
const snapshot = Y.snapshot(doc);
const compacted = Y.encodeStateAsUpdate(doc, snapshot);
```

### Implementation Complexity

**Moderate complexity (library handles hard parts):**

```javascript
// Application code is straightforward
import * as Y from 'yjs';
import { WebsocketProvider } from 'y-websocket';

// Setup
const doc = new Y.Doc();
const ytext = doc.getText('content');
const provider = new WebsocketProvider('ws://localhost:1234', 'room', doc);

// Bind to editor
ytext.observe(event => {
  // Update UI with changes
  editor.setValue(ytext.toString());
});

editor.on('change', (delta) => {
  // Apply local changes
  doc.transact(() => {
    ytext.delete(delta.start, delta.deleteLength);
    ytext.insert(delta.start, delta.text);
  });
});

// Offline support built-in
provider.on('status', ({ status }) => {
  if (status === 'disconnected') {
    // Continue working offline
    // Changes will sync automatically when reconnected
  }
});
```

**Library complexity (internal):**
- Sophisticated data structures (skip lists, trees)
- Optimized encoding/decoding
- Garbage collection strategies
- But well-tested and maintained

**Advantages:**
- Application code is simple
- Library handles complexity
- Good documentation and examples
- Active community

### Offline Support

**First-class offline support:**

```javascript
// Persistence layer
import { IndexeddbPersistence } from 'y-indexeddb';

const doc = new Y.Doc();
const persistence = new IndexeddbPersistence('document-id', doc);

// Automatically persists to IndexedDB
persistence.on('synced', () => {
  console.log('Local changes persisted');
});

// Network provider
const wsProvider = new WebsocketProvider('ws://server', 'room', doc);

// Works seamlessly:
// 1. User edits offline → saved to IndexedDB
// 2. User comes online → wsProvider syncs automatically
// 3. Conflicts resolved via CRDT properties
```

**Sync protocol:**
```javascript
// Efficient sync after offline period
class YjsSyncProtocol {
  async sync(doc, remoteDoc) {
    // 1. Exchange state vectors (compact summary of what each peer has)
    const localState = Y.encodeStateVector(doc);
    const remoteState = Y.encodeStateVector(remoteDoc);

    // 2. Send only missing updates
    const missingLocal = Y.encodeStateAsUpdate(doc, remoteState);
    const missingRemote = Y.encodeStateAsUpdate(remoteDoc, localState);

    // 3. Apply updates
    Y.applyUpdate(doc, missingRemote);
    Y.applyUpdate(remoteDoc, missingLocal);

    // Guaranteed convergence
  }
}
```

**Real-world offline scenarios:**
```
Scenario 1: Brief disconnection (seconds to minutes)
- Changes queued in memory
- Automatic reconnection and sync
- User barely notices

Scenario 2: Extended offline (hours to days)
- Changes persisted to IndexedDB
- Document remains fully functional
- Sync on reconnection (typically <1 second for reasonable edits)

Scenario 3: Concurrent offline editing
- Multiple users edit offline
- All come online at different times
- All changes merge correctly
- No data loss
```

### Real-World Examples

**Y.js ecosystem:**
- **Tiptap**: Rich text editor using Y.js
- **BlockNote**: Notion-like editor
- **Jupyter**: Collaborative notebooks
- **Nimbus Note**: Note-taking app

**Production experiences:**

```javascript
// Typical production setup
import * as Y from 'yjs';
import { WebrtcProvider } from 'y-webrtc';
import { IndexeddbPersistence } from 'y-indexeddb';

const doc = new Y.Doc();

// Local persistence
const indexeddb = new IndexeddbPersistence('doc-id', doc);

// WebRTC for peer-to-peer (low latency)
const webrtc = new WebrtcProvider('room-name', doc, {
  signaling: ['wss://signaling-server.com']
});

// Fallback to WebSocket server
const websocket = new WebsocketProvider('wss://sync-server.com', 'room', doc, {
  connect: !webrtc.connected
});

// Handles:
// - Peer-to-peer when possible (lowest latency)
// - Server relay when needed (reliability)
// - Offline persistence (local-first)
// - Automatic reconnection
```

**Reported metrics:**
- Handles 100+ concurrent users smoothly
- Documents up to several MB
- Offline periods of days/weeks
- Sync times typically <1 second
- Memory usage 5-10x better than pure CRDT

**Challenges encountered:**
- Initial learning curve for CRDT concepts
- Debugging distributed state can be tricky
- Need to design UI for concurrent edits
- Occasional interleaving in high-concurrency scenarios

